---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import StepExplanation from '../components/StepExplanation.astro';
import {
	installGuides,
	dockerfileLineSteps,
	composeBuildLineSteps,
	composeImageLineSteps,
	storageTypeSteps,
	networkTypeSteps,
	dependsOnSteps,
	withoutDependsOnTips,
	withDependsOnTips,
} from '../data/deployment';
---

<Layout>
	<div class="deployment-container">
		<h1>Deployment</h1>
		<p class="intro">
			This page explains how to deploy the Martin Homelab site with Docker or Docker Compose.
			Use the method that fits your environment best.
		</p>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>Install Docker Engine</h2>
			<p class="intro install-intro">
				Click the operating system that matches your server. The installation steps depend
				on the distribution, so Debian, Ubuntu, Raspberry Pi OS, and other systems each have
				their own instructions.
			</p>

			<div class="install-grid">
				{installGuides.map((guide) => (
					<details class="install-card">
						<summary>
							<span>{guide.title}</span>
						</summary>
						<div class="install-card__body">
							<p>{guide.summary}</p>
							<ol>
								{guide.steps.map((step) => <li>{step}</li>)}
							</ol>
							<pre><code>{guide.commands}</code></pre>
						</div>
					</details>
				))}
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>Post-installation setup</h2>
			<p>
				These are the post-installation steps from Docker's Linux guide. They cover
				non-root usage, service startup on boot, logging, and what to do next.
			</p>

			<div class="post-install-grid">
				<div class="post-install-card">
					<h3>Manage Docker as a non-root user</h3>
					<p>
						Docker runs as root and the daemon uses a Unix socket. To use Docker without
						`sudo`, create the <code>docker</code> group if needed, add your user to it,
						log out and back in, and verify with a test container.
					</p>
					<ol>
						<li>Create the <code>docker</code> group if it does not already exist.</li>
						<li>Add your user to the <code>docker</code> group.</li>
						<li>Log out and back in, or use <code>newgrp docker</code>.</li>
						<li>Test access with <code>docker run hello-world</code>.</li>
					</ol>
					<pre><code>sudo groupadd docker
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
newgrp docker
docker run hello-world</code></pre>
				</div>

				<div class="post-install-card">
					<h3>Configure Docker to start on boot with systemd</h3>
					<p>
						On Debian and Ubuntu, Docker starts on boot by default. On other systemd-based
						Linux distributions, enable both the Docker and containerd services.
					</p>
					<ol>
						<li>Enable the Docker service to start automatically.</li>
						<li>Enable containerd if your distribution requires it.</li>
						<li>Use <code>disable</code> if you later want to turn auto-start off.</li>
					</ol>
					<pre><code>sudo systemctl enable docker.service
sudo systemctl enable containerd.service</code></pre>
				</div>

				<div class="post-install-card">
					<h3>Configure default logging driver</h3>
					<p>
						Docker's default logging driver is <code>json-file</code>. Docker recommends
						considering log rotation, another logging driver such as <code>local</code>, or a
						remote log aggregator to avoid filling the disk over time.
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Enable log rotation for <code>json-file</code>.</li>
						<li>Use another driver such as <code>local</code> if it fits your setup.</li>
						<li>Send logs to a remote logging system if needed.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>

				<div class="post-install-card">
					<h3>Next steps</h3>
					<p>
						After post-installation, Docker points to the Docker workshop as a good next step
						for learning how to build images and run containers.
					</p>
					<p>
						See the official guide:
						<a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/linux-postinstall/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Linux post-installation steps for Docker Engine</a>
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>Deploy with Docker Run</h2>
			<ol>
				<li>Build the image from the project root.</li>
				<li>Start the container with <code>docker run</code> and publish the web port.</li>
				<li>Open the exposed port in your browser.</li>
			</ol>

			<pre><code>docker build -t martin-homelab .
docker run --rm -d --name martin-homelab -p 8080:80 martin-homelab</code></pre>

			<p>
				The container can be stopped later with <code>docker stop martin-homelab</code> and
				removed with <code>docker rm martin-homelab</code>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>Example Dockerfile</h2>
			<p>
				This Dockerfile builds the Astro site in one stage and serves the static files with
				Nginx in the final image.
			</p>

			<pre><code>FROM node:22-alpine AS build
WORKDIR /app

COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci

COPY . .
RUN npm run build

FROM nginx:1.27-alpine
COPY --from=build /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html

EXPOSE 80
CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]</code></pre>

			<StepExplanation title="What each line does" items={dockerfileLineSteps} />
		</section>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>Example docker-compose.yml</h2>
			<p>
				This Compose file starts the website container, builds the image from the local
				Dockerfile, and publishes the site on port 8080.
			</p>

			<pre><code>services:
  web:
    build:
      context: .
      dockerfile: Dockerfile
    ports:
      - "8080:80"
    restart: unless-stopped</code></pre>

			<StepExplanation title="What each line does" items={composeBuildLineSteps} />
		</section>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>Example docker-compose.yml without a Dockerfile</h2>
			<p>
				This example uses a prebuilt Nginx image and serves the already-built <code>dist</code>
				folder directly. It is useful when you already have static files and do not want a
				custom Dockerfile.
			</p>
			<p>
				You do not need a <code>version</code> field here because modern Docker Compose uses the
				Compose Specification directly. The file works without it, and leaving it out avoids an
				obsolete setting that does not change how this example runs.
			</p>

			<pre><code>services:
  web:
    image: nginx:1.27-alpine
    ports:
      - "8080:80"
    volumes:
      - ./dist:/usr/share/nginx/html:ro
    restart: unless-stopped</code></pre>

			<StepExplanation title="What each line does" items={composeImageLineSteps} />
		</section>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>Volumes and networks in docker-compose.yml</h2>
			<p>
				Volumes and networks solve two different problems. Volumes store data so it survives
				container restarts. Networks connect containers so they can talk to each other.
			</p>

			<StepExplanation
				title="Different ways to store data"
				items={storageTypeSteps}
				outro="For a website, a bind mount is often the simplest choice because it can serve the already-built files directly from <code>./dist</code>. For databases, a named volume is often better because Docker manages the persistent storage for you."
			/>

			<StepExplanation title="Different ways to connect containers" items={networkTypeSteps}>
				<pre><code>services:
  web:
    image: nginx:1.27-alpine
    networks:
      - front
      - back
  db:
    image: postgres:16-alpine
    networks:
      - back

networks:
  front:
  back:
    internal: true</code></pre>

				<p>
					In this example, <code>web</code> can talk to <code>db</code> on the <code>back</code>
					network, but only <code>web</code> is attached to <code>front</code>. That pattern is
					useful when you want to keep the database private and expose only the public service.
				</p>
			</StepExplanation>
		</section>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>How to make one service depend on another</h2>
			<p>
				Use <code>depends_on</code> when one service should start before another service.
				This is common when a website needs a database, or when an app needs a cache or queue
				service to be present first.
			</p>

			<pre><code>services:
  web:
    image: nginx:1.27-alpine
    depends_on:
      - db
    ports:
      - "8080:80"

  db:
    image: postgres:16-alpine
    environment:
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: example</code></pre>

			<StepExplanation
				title="What this example means"
				items={dependsOnSteps}
				outro="Important detail: <code>depends_on</code> controls startup order, but it does not wait until the other service is fully ready to accept connections. If your app must wait for a database to finish starting, add a <code>healthcheck</code> and use a startup check in the application or entrypoint."
			/>
		</section>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>When to use two services without depends_on vs with depends_on</h2>
			<p>
				Use this simple rule: if both services can start independently, do not use
				<code>depends_on</code>. If one service needs another service to start first,
				use <code>depends_on</code>.
			</p>

			<StepExplanation
				title="Use two services without depends_on"
				intro="Choose this when services are separate tools and neither one is required for the other to start."
				items={withoutDependsOnTips}
				listType="ul"
			>
				<pre><code>services:
  web:
    image: nginx:1.27-alpine
    ports:
      - "8080:80"

  backup:
    image: alpine:3.20
    command: ["sh", "-c", "while true; do echo backup; sleep 3600; done"]</code></pre>
			</StepExplanation>

			<StepExplanation
				title="Use two services with depends_on"
				intro="Choose this when one service needs another service as a dependency during startup."
				items={withDependsOnTips}
				listType="ul"
			>
				<pre><code>services:
  app:
    image: my-app:latest
    depends_on:
      - db

  db:
    image: postgres:16-alpine
    environment:
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: example</code></pre>
			</StepExplanation>
		</section>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>Deploy with Docker Compose</h2>
			<p>
				Use <code>docker compose up -d</code> to start the stack in the background.
				Use <code>docker compose up --build -d</code> when the image or Dockerfile changed
				and Compose should rebuild before starting. Use <code>docker compose -f path/to/docker-compose.yml up -d</code>
				when the compose file is in another directory or has a different file name.
				Use <code>docker compose -f nextcloud.yml up -d</code> for a compose file named
				<code>nextcloud.yml</code>.
			</p>

			<ol>
				<li>Make sure your <code>docker-compose.yml</code> file is in the project folder.</li>
				<li>Use <code>docker compose up -d</code> for the normal background start.</li>
				<li>Use <code>docker compose up --build -d</code> when the image changed and needs a rebuild first.</li>
				<li>Use <code>docker compose -f path/to/docker-compose.yml up -d</code> when you need to point Compose to a specific file.</li>
				<li>Use <code>docker compose -f nextcloud.yml up -d</code> when your compose file is named <code>nextcloud.yml</code>.</li>
				<li>Use <code>docker compose down</code> when you want to stop the stack and remove the containers and network.</li>
				<li>Use the mapped port to reach the site in your browser.</li>
			</ol>

			<pre><code># Nextcloud examples
docker compose up -d
docker compose up --build -d
docker compose -f path/to/docker-compose.yml up -d
docker compose -f nextcloud.yml up -d</code></pre>

			<p>
				Typical cases are: normal background start, use <code>docker compose up -d</code>;
				first run after image or Dockerfile changes, use <code>docker compose up --build -d</code>;
				use <code>-f</code> if you need a specific compose file path; use
				<code>docker compose down</code> to stop and remove the stack; use
				<code>docker compose -f nextcloud.yml up -d</code> when the compose file is named nextcloud.yml.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="deployment-section">
			<h2>Suggested production flow</h2>
			<p>
				For a stable deployment, build the site first, run it inside a container,
				and place a reverse proxy in front of it if you need HTTPS or a custom domain.
			</p>
		</section>
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
	.deployment-container {
		max-width: 900px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 3rem 2rem;
		color: var(--text);
		line-height: 1.7;
	}

	h1 {
		font-size: 2.5rem;
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
		color: var(--text);
	}

	.intro {
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}

	.deployment-section {
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}

	.deployment-section h2 {
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--text);
	}

	.install-intro {
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
	}

	.install-grid {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1rem;
	}

	.install-card {
		border: 1px solid rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.12);
		border-radius: 0.9rem;
		background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.03);
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	.install-card summary {
		list-style: none;
		cursor: pointer;
		padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
		display: flex;
		justify-content: space-between;
		align-items: center;
		font-weight: 700;
		color: var(--text);
	}

	.install-card summary::-webkit-details-marker {
		display: none;
	}

	.install-card__body {
		padding: 0 1.2rem 1.2rem;
		color: var(--text-muted);
	}

	ol {
		padding-left: 1.5rem;
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
	}

	li {
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
	}

	.install-card pre {
		margin: 1rem 0 0;
	}

	.post-install-grid {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1rem;
	}

	.post-install-card {
		padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
		border: 1px solid rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.12);
		border-radius: 0.9rem;
		background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.03);
	}

	.post-install-card h3 {
		margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
		font-size: 1.15rem;
		color: var(--text);
	}

	.post-install-card p,
	.post-install-card li {
		color: var(--text-muted);
	}

	.post-install-card ol,
	.post-install-card ul {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.post-install-card a {
		color: var(--accent);
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.post-install-card a:hover {
		text-decoration: underline;
	}

	pre {
		overflow-x: auto;
		padding: 1rem;
		border-radius: 0.75rem;
		background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.06);
		border: 1px solid rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.08);
	}

	code {
		font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Monaco, Consolas, monospace;
	}
</style>
